SUMMARY:

The manuscript is factually sound and well-composed. Every material claim traces directly to the admitted Grist source record; attribution chaining to The Canadian Press is transparent and appropriate; the prose is specific, non-repetitive, and free of template padding; and the headline and lede faithfully represent the story. The deterministic standards review returned `publishable: true` with both findings marked `standards-cleared`. One bounded defect prevents publication: the frontmatter correction record declares that body-copy URLs were canonicalized from the uppercase path variant to the admitted lowercase form, but all six inline Grist links in the body still carry the uppercase `/Indigenous/` path. The correction was documented but not executed in the body. Pipeline gates have not fully cleared (`gates_clear: false`).

---

FINDINGS:

The body contains six hyperlinks pointing to `https://grist.org/Indigenous/thousands-flee-as-first-nations-bear-the-brunt-of-canadas-wildfires/` (uppercase I). The admitted reference in both the frontmatter and the research packet uses the lowercase form: `https://grist.org/indigenous/...`. The frontmatter correction record (id: `thousands-flee-as-first-nations-bear-the-brunt-of-correction-2026-07-18-d2b2d7e6905b`) explicitly states its purpose is to "restore the exact source identifier without changing the prose" and lists "article" as an affected surface. That correction has not been applied to the body copy.

Beyond this defect, the piece performs well on every editorial standard subject to review. No claim is invented. No causal relationship is asserted beyond what the source supports. Named Nations are never flattened into a generic "Indigenous communities" â€” the manuscript consistently names Namaygoosisagagun, Whitesand First Nation, Kiashke Zaaging Anishinaabek, and Animbiigoo Zaagi'igan Anishinaabek by their specific names. Quotes are attributed to their originating speakers and, where appropriate, to The Canadian Press as the intermediary source, which is transparent and appropriate given that Grist is the admitted source and itself attributes those statements to CP. No SEO phrase is forced into the prose as grammar. The single-source structure is appropriate: three of the four research-packet sources are topically unrelated (a Carbon Brief piece on air conditioning, a Guardian video of a train crew, a Grist Pacific seabed mining piece), and the manuscript correctly excludes them.

---

CLAIM AUDIT:

All claims were checked against the Grist source summary provided in the research packet.

"Largely destroyed by wildfire in under an hour on Wednesday" â€” supported; source reads "largely destroy its entire community in less than an hour."

"More than two dozen people fled by boat with minutes to spare" â€” supported; source reads "more than two dozen people fled by boat just as the flames approached." The manuscript's "minutes to spare" is a slight compression of the source's "having only minutes to gather their belongings"; it is accurate in force.

"Thirteen Nations... forced 2,182 people from their homes" â€” supported verbatim from source.

"903 wildfires... 750 out of control, 43 started within the prior 24 hours" â€” supported verbatim from source.

"Seven First Nations have fled the 193 fires now burning there" â€” supported verbatim from source.

Named evacuating communities â€” supported from source.

Debassige quote ("It is with heartfelt sadness...") â€” supported verbatim.

"Residents did not receive advance warning from provincial or national officials, nor did they receive evacuation support from outside" â€” supported; source attributes this to Debassige's statement to The Canadian Press.

Recognition-gap legal analysis â€” supported; source explicitly explains the distinction between residents recognized under the Indian Act and the community lacking that recognition at the organizational level.

Daniel letter quotes â€” supported verbatim.

Eric Head spokesperson statement â€” supported from source.

Benedict quotes â€” supported verbatim.

Assembly of First Nations emergency resolutions â€” supported from source.

Anishinabek Nation prayer statement â€” supported verbatim.

Closing Daniel letter quote ("The question is not merely whether recovery will be delayed...") â€” supported verbatim.

No invented causal claims were found. No claim outran its source.

---

REQUIRED REPAIRS:

Update all six inline body-copy hyperlinks from `https://grist.org/Indigenous/...` to `https://grist.org/indigenous/...` (lowercase i) to match the admitted canonical URL and fully execute the documented correction. No changes to prose, structure, or claims are required.

---

PIPELINE EVIDENCE:

The deterministic standards gate cleared `attention-preserve-correction` with the message "hidden correction state corrected," apparently treating the presence of the correction frontmatter record as sufficient evidence that the correction had been applied to the body. The body text contradicts that determination. The gate verified that correction metadata existed; it did not verify that the corrected URL string actually appears in the body copy in its canonical form. This is a narrow implementation gap: the correction's `affected_surfaces` field lists "article," but the gate did not diff body-copy link targets against the admitted reference URL.

The research packet warrant is correctly scoped as source-bound intake. The three off-topic sources are consistent with normal search-score noise and their presence in the packet is not a pipeline defect. The `demand-lens-missing-h2` warning is contextually inapplicable to this breaking-news format and the standards gate correctly cleared it.

---

PIPELINE IMPROVEMENT:

The correction verification step in the deterministic gate should diff body-copy hyperlinks against each admitted reference URL in the frontmatter, not merely confirm that a correction record exists. Any correction listing "article" as an affected surface should trigger a mechanical check confirming the canonical URL string is present in the body in its admitted form before marking the correction state as cleared. A warning-level signal at that step would have caught the `/Indigenous/` vs. `/indigenous/` discrepancy before editorial review and made the `draft-repair` replay unnecessary.

